#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163031} :activity/id #uuid "9c611ad4-705d-5751-a473-84f3e834b6d3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049503 :topic/id #uuid "fa18866e-f310-5b54-8bcb-21e9073c9cde" :topic/math-academy-id 3134} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163031 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163306 :tutorial/content "The invertible matrix theorem tells us that if $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix with full rank (i.e., $\\mathrm{rank}(A)=n$), then there exists a unique inverse matrix $A^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$AA^{-1}=A^{-1}\\!A=I_n,$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ denotes the $n \\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nThere are two questions that we can ask:\n\n- Can we extend the notion of an inverse matrix to one that works for $n\\times n$ matrices $A$ where $\\mathrm{rank}(A) < n?$\n- Is it possible to generalize the concept of an inverse to non-square matrices?\n\nThe answer to both questions is yes! This generalization of the inverse of $A$ is called the **pseudoinverse** of $A,$ and is denoted $A^+.$ The dimensions of $A^+$ are the same as $A^T.$\n\nAs an example, let's consider the ${\\color{red}{3}}\\times {\\color{blue}{2}}$ matrix $A$ and its corresponding pseudoinverse, given below:\n\n$$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\qquad A^{+} = \\dfrac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]$$\n\n- Computing $A^{+}\\!A,$ we have $$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+}\\,A & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$ which is the identity matrix $I_{\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n- Computing $A\\!A^{+},$ we have $$\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{+} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which is *almost* the identity matrix $I_{\\color{red}{3}}.$\n\nWe can explain this result using the following properties of the pseudoinverse:\n\n- *If the columns of an $m\\times {\\color{blue}{n}}$ matrix $A$ are linearly independent, then $A^+A = I_{\\color{blue}{n}}.$*\n- *If the rows of an ${\\color{red}{m}}\\times n$ matrix $A$ are linearly independent, then $AA^+ = I_{\\color{red}{m}}.$*\n\nIn the example above, the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, but the rows are not.\n\nMoreover, the notion of a pseudoinverse is consistent with inverse matrices. In particular,\n\n*If $A$ is an invertible square matrix, then $A^{+} = A^{-1}.$*\n\nThis lesson will discuss how to compute the pseudoinverse for any matrix, starting with an important special case." :tutorial/id #uuid "6cec9ecf-529c-566f-9895-3fde09f24253" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6074 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a081b9a8-d058-5b79-8d8b-a8471a1f524a" :step/math-academy-id 36962 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161902 :question/id #uuid "e0a12130-2908-58fd-a8e0-91083fa30e96" :question/math-academy-id "e-9828"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e82e8b16-51a8-59c8-9ed1-51619335de97" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048143}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388545}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "b8379f09-2e11-58eb-bae4-01a9b4bd2b5b" :step/math-academy-id 36963 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163034}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162090 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161903 :question/id #uuid "64e7f84f-da34-5ae2-b2d1-72373a1d2888" :question/math-academy-id "e-9829"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3460bfd6-8b2f-5a52-891a-4c8b2af5b5e8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388531}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Diagonal Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "a1fe2653-29e8-5527-b4fa-083f233b81c0" :step/math-academy-id 36966 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163046}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163307 :tutorial/content "Consider the matrix\n\n$$A= [\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],$$\n\nand its singular value decomposition $A=U \\Sigma V ^T,$ where\n\n$$U = [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & 0 & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\quad \\Sigma = [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\quad V = [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nNotice that $\\Sigma$ contains a row of zeros. In these situations, it's possible to simplify the decomposition of $A.$ This simplified decomposition is called the **reduced singular value decomposition** of $A.$\n\nFirst, we note that $A$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix. In addition, the matrix $\\Sigma$ has two non-zero singular values on its main diagonal, so the rank of $A$ is $\\color{blue}r=2.$\n\nTo find our reduced SVD, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we remove all zero rows and columns from $\\Sigma.$ In our example, we should delete the $3$rd row since it contains only zeros. Notice that we obtain a square invertible matrix of rank ${\\color{blue}2}{:}$ $$\\Sigma=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\qquad \\longrightarrow \\qquad \\Sigma_r= [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$$\n- Next, we remove all extra columns of $U$ starting from the right, keeping only $\\color{blue}2$ columns. In our example, we should delete the $3$rd column:\n$$U=\\left[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & 0 & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right] \\qquad \\longrightarrow \\qquad U_r=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$$\n- Finally, we remove all extra columns of $V$ starting from the right, keeping only $\\color{blue}2$ columns. In our example, we should keep $V$ as it is since it already has only $2$ columns: $$V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\qquad \\longrightarrow \\qquad V_r=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nThe reduced singular value decomposition of $A$ is then given by\n\n$$A = U_r \\Sigma_r V_r^T.$$\n\nSo, in our example, we have\n\n$$A= [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]^T.$$\n\nIn general, a singular value decomposition\n\n$$A=U \\Sigma V^T$$\n\nof an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ is not unique. However, we can always simplify it to the corresponding reduced singular value decomposition\n\n$$A=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r^T$$\n\nwith the following properties:\n\n- $\\Sigma_r$ is a square diagonal matrix of size $r\\times r,$ where $r\\leq \\min\\{m,n\\}$ is the rank of $A.$ The main diagonal of $\\Sigma_r$ contains the non-zero singular values $\\sigma_1, \\sigma_2, \\ldots, \\sigma_r$ of $A$ in descending order.\n- $U_r$ is a $m\\times r$ matrix obtained by keeping only the first $r$ columns of $U.$\n- $V_r$ is a $n \\times r$ matrix obtained by keeping only the first $r$ columns of $V.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "924b4229-0b49-5d81-9255-b9be0ec07ab6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6075 :tutorial/title "Reduced Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "0c540186-c349-53ef-8094-366eb44f526a" :step/math-academy-id 36969 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163035}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162091 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161904 :question/id #uuid "66c34c76-6243-50b8-832f-027abb6ab736" :question/math-academy-id "e-9830"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff858037-da99-53e9-acdc-e4963e7d765e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388566}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Reduced Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "03964208-b7f5-5c5d-ab41-385b66f7efbd" :step/math-academy-id 36970 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163039}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163039 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163309 :tutorial/content "Recall that the least-squares solution of the system\n\n$$A\\mathbf x=\\mathbf{b},$$\n\nis a vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that $\\| A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\|$ is minimized.\n\nThe least-squares solution is not unique when $A$ has linearly dependent columns. However, it can be shown that a so-called **optimal least-squares solution** $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}$ always exists. The optimal least-squares solution is the solution to the least squares problem with the shortest possible length.\n\nIt turns out that the optimal least-squares solution can be found using the pseudoinverse of $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\\mathbf{x^\\ast} = A^{+} \\mathbf{b}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6f731acb-e452-5433-88a5-1e2c66fd5a84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6078 :tutorial/title "The Optimal Least-Squares Solution"} :step/id #uuid "f03c737f-a30a-5ef1-8ade-d5c1bd49e933" :step/math-academy-id 36979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163313 :tutorial/content "There are many ways to find the pseudoinverse of a given matrix $A.$ One way is to use the singular value decomposition of $A{:}$\n\nSpecifically, if $$A=U \\Sigma V ^T$$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by $$A^+ = V \\, \\Sigma^+ \\, U^T,$$ where $\\Sigma^+$ is the pseudoinverse of $\\Sigma.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bd110f79-cfe0-58f8-b7e6-ea9baa7814d9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6102 :tutorial/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse Using Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "cef3aa4e-1b1d-50ea-b285-5cc2dcac6f73" :step/math-academy-id 37091 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163032}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163314 :tutorial/content "For an *almost* zero ${\\color{red}{m}}\\times {\\color{blue}{n}}$ matrix with non-zero entries\n\n$$\\sigma_1, \\: \\sigma_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: \\sigma_r$$\n\non the main diagonal only, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the *almost* zero ${\\color{blue}{n}}\\times {\\color{red}{m}}$ matrix with non-zero entries\n\n$${\\sigma_1^{-1}}, \\, {\\sigma_2^{-1}}, \\, \\ldots, \\, {\\sigma_r^{-1}}$$\n\non the main diagonal only.\n\nFor example, for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$A = [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nwe calculate its pseudoinverse as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Replace the non-zero elements on the main diagonal with their multiplicative inverses.\n- **Step 2:** Transpose the resulting matrix.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{-1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {4}^{-1} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}1/3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1/4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8709222e-61fc-512e-82ff-3564c1513100" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6103 :tutorial/title "The Pseudoinverse of an Almost Diagonal Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e5289df4-d145-54b7-a6f3-e8e13a522ee7" :step/math-academy-id 37092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163033}}] :activity/title "Singular Value Decomposition and the Pseudoinverse Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162228}]}
